.he "ps (1)                       9/30/26                       ps (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
ps
- process status
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
ps [ -alx ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Ps
reports on the processes in the system's process table: what each is
running, whose it is, what it is waiting for, and how much memory it
holds.
.sp
With no options it lists the processes attached to the terminal ps was
run from - the login and what that session has started - which is what
a user asking "what is running" almost always means.  The options widen
that, and one of them lengthens the listing.
.sp
The table is read out of
.bd
/dev/mem.
Ps seeks to 0x1003, where the kernel leaves the address of the table
for it, and reads the whole table from there, so what it reports is the
live table and not a copy.  Any user may see other users' command
names.  The data those commands are processing is not readable this
way.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-a		All.  Also list the processes attached to other
		terminals.  Without it, a process whose controlling
		terminal is not ps's own is skipped.
.sp
.ti -10
-l		Long listing.  Print the eleven columns below rather
		than the default three.
.sp
.ti -10
-x		Also list the processes with no controlling terminal -
		those the kernel and init run detached from any tty.
		Used alone it lists only those, since the processes
		that do have a terminal are still skipped.
.sp
.ti -10
-p		Accepted and ignored.  See NOTES.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
COLUMNS
.in +5
.br
The default listing is
.bd
PID, COMMAND
and
.bd
TTY.
With
.bd
-l
it is
.bd
PID, COMMAND, TTY, UID, PAR, SIZ, NIC, PRI, WAIT, STATE
and
.bd
PC.
.sp
.in +5
.ti -5
PID	The process id.
.sp
.ti -5
COMMAND	The name the process was started with, as the kernel recorded
	it, cut to eight characters.  The two processes that have no
	name of their own print what they are: pid 0 is System and pid 1
	is Init.  A process that has died but whose entry has not been
	reused prints DEFUNCT.
.sp
.ti -5
TTY	The controlling terminal, found by device number in a scan of
	/dev and printed under the name that directory gives it.  A
	process with no terminal prints blanks; a device number that is
	not in /dev prints ?.
.sp
.ti -5
UID	The user id the process is running as.
.sp
.ti -5
PAR	The parent's process id, or 0 for pid 0, which has none.
.sp
.ti -5
SIZ	The memory the process holds, in kilobytes: the number of 4K
	segments its entry names, times four.  pid 0 prints 64, which is
	the kernel's own size rather than a measurement.
.sp
.ti -5
NIC	The user's say in the process's priority: 128 minus the nice
	value, so an unniced process prints 128.
.sp
.ti -5
PRI	The priority the scheduler is using for it, which is not the
	same number the user sets.
.sp
.ti -5
WAIT	The address of the event the process is sleeping on, in hex.
	It is an address in the kernel, and two processes waiting on
	the same thing print the same one.
.sp
.ti -5
STATE	Six flags, two characters each, in the order below.  A flag
	that is set prints its pair, and one that is clear prints -- in
	its place, so the field is always twelve characters and the
	position of a pair is what says which flag it is.
.sp
.ti -5
PC	The process's program counter, read out of its user structure:
	where it was when it was last stopped, which for a process that
	is running is the last switch away from it.  A process that is
	not in memory prints 0, since there is no user structure to
	read - so a 0 here means swapped, not stopped at address 0.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
STATE FLAGS
.in +5
.br
.in +5
  Al	Alive.  The process has not terminated.
.br
  Aw	Awake.  It is not waiting for a slow event and may be run at
	once.
.br
  Ld	Loaded.  The image is in memory.
.br
  Sw	Swapped.  The image is on the swap device.
.br
  Lk	Locked.  The image may not be swapped out.
.br
  Sy	System.  The process is in its system phase, inside the
	kernel.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The exit status is the opposite of what a shell expects, and that is
carried over from the original rather than fixed here: ps exits 1 after
a successful listing, and 0 when it cannot read the table.  It cannot
read the table when
.bd
/dev/mem
cannot be opened or read, and it names that file on the standard error
first.  A shell script that tests the status of ps gets it backwards.
.sp
-p is accepted and does nothing.  The original had it to add the PC to
the short listing; here the PC is always in the long listing and there
is no short form of it.
.sp
ps is built against this kernel's
.bd
<sys/proc.h>
and reads the table through it.  A change to
.bd
struct proc
changes the size ps reads and the offset of every field after the one
that changed, so ps has to be rebuilt with the kernel it is to read:
an older ps binary is not a reader of a newer table.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/bin/ps
.br
/dev/mem
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
kill (1), sh (1), signal (2)
